---
import Public from '../layouts/Public.astro';
import LegalDoc from '../components/legal/LegalDoc.astro';

const title = 'Privacy Policy';
const lastUpdated = 'Last updated: 27 November 2026';
const description = 'Privacy Policy of ChipMates gemeinnützige GmbH for the Agora Cosmica platform. GDPR-compliant information on data processing, processors, and the rights of data subjects.';
---
<Public
  lang="en"
  title={title}
  fullTitle="Privacy Policy | Agora Cosmica"
  description={description}
  canonicalPath="/privacy"
  noHreflang
>
  <LegalDoc title={title} lastUpdated={lastUpdated} lang="en">
    <section>
      <p>
        This is the English translation of our German privacy notice (Datenschutzerklärung). ChipMates gemeinnützige GmbH is a German company, so the German version is legally authoritative. We provide this English version so English-speaking users can read the notice in their own language. If anything differs, the <a href="/datenschutz/">German version</a> prevails.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Preamble</h2>
      <p>
        With this privacy notice we want to explain to you which types of your personal data (also referred to below simply as "data") we process, for which purposes, and to what extent. This notice applies to all processing of personal data carried out by us, both in the provision of our services and in particular on our websites, in mobile applications, and within external online presences such as our social media profiles (collectively the "online offering").
      </p>
      <p>The terms used are not gender-specific.</p>
      <p>
        <strong>Contents</strong><br/>
        * Preamble<br/>
        * Controller<br/>
        * Overview of processing<br/>
        * Relevant legal bases<br/>
        * Security measures<br/>
        * International data transfers<br/>
        * Rights of data subjects<br/>
        * Cookies and local storage<br/>
        * Provision of the online offering and web hosting<br/>
        * Storage on your device<br/>
        * Processors<br/>
        * Conversion measurement (Google Ads)<br/>
        * Reach measurement<br/>
        * Dispute resolution
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Controller</h2>
      <p>
        ChipMates gemeinnützige GmbH<br/>
        represented by Michael Strasser<br/>
        Schusterstr. 50<br/>
        79098 Freiburg im Breisgau, Germany<br/>
        Email: <a href="mailto:chipmates@chipmates.ai">chipmates@chipmates.ai</a>
      </p>
      <p>
        No data protection officer has been appointed, as the legal requirements for this are not met (Sect. 38 BDSG, the German Federal Data Protection Act).
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Overview of processing</h2>
      <p>
        The following overview summarizes the types of data processed and the purposes of their processing, and refers to the data subjects.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Types of data processed:</strong><br/>
        * Contact data<br/>
        * Content data<br/>
        * Usage data<br/>
        * Meta, communication, and procedural data
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Categories of data subjects:</strong><br/>
        * Communication partners<br/>
        * Users
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Purposes of processing:</strong><br/>
        * Contact requests and communication<br/>
        * Security measures<br/>
        * Management of and response to inquiries<br/>
        * Feedback<br/>
        * Provision of our online offering and usability<br/>
        * Information technology infrastructure
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Relevant legal bases</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Relevant legal bases under the GDPR:</strong> Below you will find an overview of the legal bases of the GDPR on which we process personal data. Please note that, in addition to the provisions of the GDPR, national data protection rules in your or our country of residence or establishment may apply. Should more specific legal bases be relevant in an individual case, we will inform you of these in this privacy notice.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Consent (Art. 6(1)(a) GDPR)</li>
        <li>Legitimate interests (Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR)</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        <strong>National data protection rules in Germany:</strong> In addition to the data protection rules of the GDPR, national data protection rules apply in Germany. This includes in particular the Federal Data Protection Act (Bundesdatenschutzgesetz, BDSG). The BDSG contains special rules on the right of access, the right to erasure, the right to object, the processing of special categories of personal data, processing for other purposes, transmission, and automated decision-making in individual cases including profiling. Data protection laws of the individual federal states may also apply.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Application of the GDPR and the Swiss FADP:</strong> This privacy notice serves to provide information both under the Swiss Federal Act on Data Protection (Swiss FADP) and under the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR). For this reason, please note that, due to the broader territorial application and for clarity, the terms of the GDPR are used. In particular, instead of the terms "processing" of "personal data", "overriding interest", and "particularly sensitive personal data" used in the Swiss FADP, the terms "processing" of "personal data", "legitimate interest", and "special categories of data" used in the GDPR are used. The legal meaning of the terms is, however, still determined in accordance with the Swiss FADP where the Swiss FADP applies.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Security measures</h2>
      <p>
        We take appropriate technical and organizational measures in accordance with the legal requirements to ensure a level of protection appropriate to the risk. These measures cover in particular safeguarding the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of data.
      </p>
      <p>
        The measures include controlling physical and electronic access to the data, safeguarding availability and segregation, and establishing procedures for exercising data subject rights, erasing data, and responding to threats to the data.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>TLS/SSL encryption (https):</strong> To protect users' data, we use TLS/SSL encryption. This ensures the secure transmission of data between our website and the user's browser.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>International data transfers</h2>
      <p>
        Where we process data in a third country (outside the EU or the EEA), this is done only in accordance with the legal requirements.
      </p>
      <p>
        Data transfers to third countries take place only where the level of data protection has been recognized by an adequacy decision, through standard contractual clauses, explicit consent, or in the context of legally required transfers.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>EU-US Trans-Atlantic Data Privacy Framework:</strong> Certain companies in the USA provide a recognized level of data protection through the Data Privacy Framework (DPF), which has been recognized as safe within the scope of an adequacy decision.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Rights of data subjects</h2>
      <p>As a data subject, you have various rights under the GDPR, including:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Right to object (Art. 21 GDPR):</strong> You have the right, on grounds relating to your particular situation, to object to the processing of your data based on Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR (legitimate interest). This concerns in particular processing for security and rate-limiting purposes.</li>
        <li>Right to withdraw consent</li>
        <li>Right of access (Art. 15 GDPR)</li>
        <li>Right to rectification (Art. 16 GDPR)</li>
        <li>Right to erasure (Art. 17 GDPR) and restriction of processing (Art. 18 GDPR)</li>
        <li>Right to data portability (Art. 20 GDPR)</li>
        <li>Right to lodge a complaint with a supervisory authority (Art. 77 GDPR)</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        <strong>Competent supervisory authority:</strong> The State Commissioner for Data Protection and Freedom of Information of Baden-Württemberg, Heilbronner Straße 35, 70191 Stuttgart, Germany, <a href="https://www.baden-wuerttemberg.datenschutz.de">www.baden-wuerttemberg.datenschutz.de</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Cookies and local storage</h2>
      <p>
        We do NOT use tracking, analytics, or marketing cookies. The only cookie use is by Cloudflare (__cf_bm, and after a security check cf_clearance), technically necessary security cookies for bot protection and firewall (Sect. 25(2) No. 2 TDDDG, the German Telecommunications-Digital-Services Data Protection Act). These cookies are set automatically by Cloudflare and require no consent.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Provision of the online offering and web hosting</h2>
      <p>
        We process users' data in order to provide our online services. For this purpose we process the user's IP address, which is necessary to transmit the content and functions of our online services to the user's browser or device.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Types of data processed:</strong><br/>
        * Usage data
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Data subjects:</strong><br/>
        * Users
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Legal bases:</strong><br/>
        * Legitimate interests (Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR)
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Web hosting:</strong> The website is delivered via Cloudflare Pages (Cloudflare, Inc., 101 Townsend St, San Francisco, CA 94107, USA) as a content delivery network. Cloudflare is certified under the EU-US Data Privacy Framework (DPF). When delivering the static website files (HTML, CSS, JavaScript), IP addresses are recorded in Cloudflare access logs. Beyond this, no user data is processed via Cloudflare Pages.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Audio files and images:</strong> The stories you can listen to are pre-recorded audio files. We deliver these files and the images from our own storage at Cloudflare (media.agoracosmica.org). As with the website, your IP address is processed to transmit the files to your browser.
      </p>
      <p>
        Data is collected on the basis of Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR. The operator has a legitimate interest in the technically error-free presentation and reliable provision of the service.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Storage on your device</h2>
      <p>
        We do not use tracking, analytics or marketing cookies. Our pages store the following in your browser.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Technically necessary for a function you use</strong> (Sect. 25(2) No. 2 TDDDG, no consent needed):
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Your progress and the state of the pages, such as where you are in a story, stories heard and read, saved sentences, and your settings for playback and display (localStorage)</li>
        <li>Language setting (localStorage)</li>
        <li>Your answer to the ad-measurement question, yes or no, with version and date, so that we can respect it (localStorage) for 12 months</li>
        <li>Short-lived data for the current tab (sessionStorage, deleted when the tab closes): interface state such as whether a note was already shown</li>
        <li>Cloudflare security cookies: __cf_bm (bot protection, 30 minutes) and, after a security check, cf_clearance</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        <strong>Only with your consent to ad measurement</strong> (Sect. 25(1) TDDDG, see "Conversion measurement"), in sessionStorage, deleted when the tab closes or when you withdraw:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>The ad's click ID</li>
        <li>Markers that stop a step from being sent to Google twice, and the seconds listened toward the 30-second step</li>
      </ul>
      <p>You can remove all of it at any time by clearing your browser data.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Processors</h2>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Provider</th>
            <th>Purpose</th>
            <th>Location</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Cloudflare, Inc.</td>
            <td>CDN and web hosting, storage for audio files and images, server for counting and ad measurement, WAF, bot protection</td>
            <td>Edge (EU-prioritized)</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Conversion measurement (Google Ads, only with your consent)</h2>
      <p>
        We run free ads through Google's program for nonprofits (Google Ad Grants). If you reach us through one of these ads, the web address contains a click ID issued by Google (gclid). Google can link it to your click and possibly to your Google account, so we treat it as personal data.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you arrive through such an ad, we may ask whether we can count your visit for Google, either right away or later in your visit, for example after a minute of listening or at the end of a poem. The question is optional, and the whole library stays open either way. If you are under 16, we ask you to say no. Until you answer, the click ID stays only in the page's memory and is not stored. If you say no, it is discarded, and your browser remembers the no. If you close the question with the X, that counts as a no for this visit: the click ID is discarded, nothing is stored and nothing is sent to Google. Your browser does not remember this no, so we ask again the next time you arrive through one of our ads. As soon as the page has read the click ID, we take it out of the address bar, so a reload carries none and a shared link does not pass it on. An empty placeholder (gclid=) stays in its place, so our anonymous count still recognises a reload as an arrival from an ad. If you say yes, your browser keeps the click ID in this tab (sessionStorage) until the tab closes, and remembers the yes (localStorage) for 12 months, so that later visits through our ads are counted too. Visitors from our paid ads (their web address contains p=1) are never asked, and their click ID is discarded at once.
      </p>
      <p>
        With your consent, our server sends Google the click ID at most twice: when you say yes to the question, and once you have heard 30 seconds of audio in total. Seconds you heard before your answer count toward the 30. If you come back later through one of our ads while your yes still holds, we do not ask again, and Google receives the new click ID at most once, after 30 seconds of audio. With each of these sends, Google receives the click ID, the step (as a conversion action), a value and a currency, the time, an order number (click ID plus step, so nothing counts twice) and a consent signal (use for measurement allowed, use for personalized advertising not allowed). Google receives neither the figure nor your country, and no other data about you. No Google script or pixel runs on our pages. The transfer goes from our server to Google (Google Ads API).
      </p>
      <p>
        Recipient: Google Ireland Limited and Google LLC (USA). A transfer to the USA can take place. Google is certified under the EU-US Data Privacy Framework. How Google uses data from partner sites: <a href="https://business.safety.google/privacy">https://business.safety.google/privacy</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Storage period:</strong> We do not keep the click ID on our servers. For each step sent, we keep a record without the click ID (the step, the figure if there was one, whether the question came at once or later, and the time) for 90 days, to check that the counting works. To prevent abuse, when steps are sent our server also keeps a hash of your IP address, made with a secret key, together with how many steps came from it in the same hour. Both are stored apart from this record and expire after at most one hour and two minutes. Google keeps the data according to its own retention rules for advertising data.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Legal basis and withdrawal:</strong> Your consent (Art. 6(1)(a) GDPR and Sect. 25(1) TDDDG). You can withdraw it at any time with effect for the future: with the "Ad measurement" link at the foot of every page. Withdrawing deletes the stored click ID and records your no. After that, nothing more is sent. The hash of your IP address that our server keeps when steps are sent rests not on your consent but on our legitimate interest in protecting the measurement from floods of reports (Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR), as described under "Reach measurement".
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Reach measurement</h2>
      <p>
        We count how our pages are used, without cookies and without identifying anyone. When something happens, for example a page opens or a story plays, the page sends a short count to our server. Each count is one row with a few coarse labels, depending on what happened: what happened (for example "story finished"), the page path, the figure, the mode or chapter, for stories the version of the text, the language (en or de), the country as a two-letter code that Cloudflare derives from the connection (for example DE, or XX when unknown), the type of device (mobile, tablet or desktop), and for some steps a rough range instead of an exact number (for example "1 to 3 minutes listened"). Our measurement documentation lists every label. This is also how we count how often the question about our ads was on screen, and how often it was answered yes or no, or closed with the X.
      </p>
      <p>
        When a visit starts, the count also notes in one coarse word what kind of place the visitor came from, for example "search", "Google ad", "AI assistant" or "direct". The page works this out from the address of the page that linked here, which browsers normally pass along, or for an ad from its mark in the address you arrive at, and keeps only that word. The same word can also go with the counts of the stories you hear or read after that. Those counts then also note what kind of page the visit began on: the front page, a figure's page, poems, or another page.
      </p>
      <p>
        When a count comes from the network of a data centre or cloud provider, our server marks it with the word "auto". This lets us read counts that may come from programs in data centres apart from the rest. The count itself is kept. Counts from people who connect through such a network, for example some VPN services, carry this word too. To do this, the server compares the number of the network, which Cloudflare passes along with every connection, with a fixed list of such providers. It keeps only the word "auto". It does not store the network number, write it to a log or pass it on.
      </p>
      <p>
        No count contains an IP address, a user ID or anything you typed. We build no profiles, and we do not recognize a browser from one visit to the next for counting. The counts are kept for 90 days.
      </p>
      <p>
        To prevent abuse, when counts arrive our server keeps a hash of your IP address, made with a secret key, together with how many counts of one kind came from it in the same hour. Both are stored apart from the counts and expire after at most one hour and two minutes.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Legal basis:</strong> Our legitimate interest in knowing whether our nonprofit service works and whom it reaches (Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR). The counts carry no identifier and cannot be traced back to a person by us, so they are not personal data (Recital 26 GDPR). We process the hash of your IP address on the basis of our legitimate interest in protecting the counting and our server from floods of reports (Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR). It is pseudonymised but still personal data, because with the key it could be traced back to an IP address. For the counting, nothing is stored on your device. The only thing read there is whether the browser carries a test marker (agc_probe), which lets us subtract our own test visits from the counts. It is a fixed value with no identifier, and your browser has it only if you opened a page with ?probe=1 in its address. What our pages store on your device for their own functions is listed under "Storage on your device".
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Dispute resolution</h2>
      <p>
        We are not willing or obliged to participate in dispute resolution proceedings before a consumer conciliation body.
      </p>
    </section>
  </LegalDoc>
</Public>
